PARTIALLY SUPERSEDED BY HW-027 -- READ THIS FIRST
------------------------------------------------
The two bugs below are real and the measurements stand. But this file's mutation table also
said, of the GDMA channel reset, "and neither is a channel reset". THAT IS FALSE. The build
it was based on never had the reset in it: I "verified" the patch with
`grep -c gdma_ll_rx_reset_channel`, got 2, and did not know that the PRISTINE driver already
contains two -- in dma_esp32_config_rx() and dma_esp32_reload(). The count proved nothing.

Re-run with the resets counted INSIDE dma_esp32_stop(), the channel reset ALONE is sufficient:
canary 0/8, 100 cycles, with the I2S RX unit left running. See
evidence/20260713-hw027-dma_stop-returns-before-the-channel-stops.log.

================================================================================

HW-024  ESP32 I2S: the leak fix I published had TWO bugs of its own -- and the harness
        I built to prove it caught them CAUGHT ONLY ONE. A human caught the other, by
        eye. This file is the corrected record.

        M5Stack StickS3 (ESP32-S3-PICO-1), Zephyr 4.4.0, 2026-07-12/13.
        Upstream: issue #113310, PR #113311.

READ THIS FIRST: THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS FILE OVERCLAIMED
------------------------------------------------------------
It said, of the four-build table below, "the test fails on both of my own bugs. That is
what makes it a test rather than a demonstration."

That was FALSE, and it was published -- in this file, in a commit message, in the body of
PR #113311, and in a comment to the Espressif area maintainer who had just asked to take
the fix into his own PR.

The harness printed `STOP drained 0 tail blocks` on the i2s_hal_tx_stop build and then
printed `STRESS PASSED`, because nothing in it ever ASSERTED on that number. `tail` was
counted, printed, and thrown away; the pass/fail conditions looked only at the mem-slab
census, the canary, and the cycle count. And the slab census could not possibly see this
bug: stopping the TX unit kills the shared bit clock, so RX never reaches STOPPING, so
nothing leaks and nothing is double-freed. Two defects, each hiding the other, behind a
flat 8/8 slab.

What actually caught it was a person reading "drained 0" next to stock Zephyr's
"drained 1" and asking why. A test that prints the evidence and does not check it is not
a test, and I said it was one.

The harness now asserts it. Re-measured, and both directions verified below.

THE BUG THE OLD HARNESS COULD NOT SEE, AND THE ONE IT COULD
-----------------------------------------------------------
    build                                  graceful STOP    mem-slab        verdict
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    stock upstream Zephyr                  drains 1/1       LEAKS 8,7,6...  FAILED
    my patch v1 (i2s_hal_tx_stop)          DRAINS 0 of 27   flat 8/8        FAILED *
    my patch v2, RX hand-off removed       drains 1/1       CORRUPT rx=9/8  FAILED
    my patch v2, complete                  drains 27/27     flat 8/8        PASSED

    * this row is the correction. With the old harness it read PASSED.

The corrected harness's verdict on the tx_stop build, verbatim:

    *** STRESS FAILED: 27 of 27 graceful STOPs drained NOTHING (first at cycle 3).
        The RX callback's STOPPING branch always hands its final block to the receive
        queue before it stops, so an empty tail read means that branch never ran and
        the RX DMA never finished the block it was holding. On this SoC that is what
        stopping the I2S TX unit does: the TX unit is the source of the shared bit
        clock, and the RX side is then stranded in I2S_STATE_STOPPING for good.

        The slab is FLAT and the canary is CLEAN and it is still broken. ***

and on the correct driver:

    === STRESS PASSED: 100 cycles -- 20 with TX starved on purpose, and 27 ended with
        a graceful I2S_TRIGGER_STOP instead of a DROP. All 27 of those drained a tail
        block (0 came back empty), the slab held 8/8 free the whole way, and the canary
        was clean. ===

WHAT THE OLD STRESS COULD NOT SEE AT ALL
-----------------------------------------
The stress that signed off the first version of the fix ran 100 cycles of
configure/START/io/DROP and passed. I2S_TRIGGER_DROP goes straight to I2S_STATE_READY
under irq_lock; I2S_TRIGGER_STOP is the only trigger that ever reaches
I2S_STATE_STOPPING, and BOTH of the bugs below live there. A test whose inputs never vary
cannot find a defect that only appears when they do.

So the stress now ends one cycle in three with a graceful STOP and a tail read -- and,
since 2026-07-13, ASSERTS that the tail read returns something.

BUG 1 -- I STOPPED THE WRONG UNIT, AND IT KILLED THE BIT CLOCK
--------------------------------------------------------------
The crash the quiesce fixes is real: on the GDMA path *_stop_transfer() called dma_stop()
and nothing else, so the hardware went on writing into a block that had just been handed
back to the slab, and the audio landed on the free-list next pointer. Stopping the I2S
unit before dma_stop() fixes it.

But I did it in BOTH directions "for symmetry", and symmetry is exactly wrong here:

  RX DMA WRITES to the mem-slab block. A burst that lands after the free corrupts the
     free list. Stopping the RX unit is necessary.
  TX DMA only READS from it. A late read of a freed block cannot move one byte of slab
     metadata. Stopping the TX unit is not necessary --

  -- and it is harmful, because on this SoC the I2S TX unit is what generates the shared
  bit clock. On a full-duplex graceful STOP the TX side stops first; the bit clock dies
  with it; the RX DMA can then never finish the block it is holding;
  i2s_esp32_rx_callback() never fires again; and the stream is stranded in
  I2S_STATE_STOPPING forever.

Measured, cycle 3, same code, three drivers:

    stock upstream          my patch v1                 my patch v2 (correct)
    c3.stop    tx=4 rx=5    c3.stop    tx=6 rx=7        c3.stop    tx=6 rx=7
    c3.stopped tx=5 rx=5    c3.stopped tx=7 rx=7        c3.stopped tx=7 rx=7
    c3.tail    tx=5 rx=6    c3.tail    tx=7 rx=7        c3.tail    tx=7 rx=8
    drained 1 tail block    DRAINED 0 TAIL BLOCKS       drained 1 tail block

rx frozen at 7 for a full 150 ms is an RX DMA that is not running. Stock recovers; my
patch did not, every single time.

Note what the pristine non-GDMA branch actually calls, which is what I thought I was
mirroring: i2s_hal_tx_stop_LINK() -- the DMA link -- not i2s_hal_tx_stop(), the unit.
I mirrored the wrong function. (Both names do appear in the pristine driver, in
*_start_transfer(), which stops and resets the unit before arming the next transfer.
That also made a bare `grep i2s_hal_rx_stop` idempotency test say "already patched"
about a completely untouched tree; that check now matches the two-line sequence.)

BUG 2 -- PART 3 TURNED THE LEAK INTO A DOUBLE FREE ON THE STOPPING PATH
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Giving *_stop_transfer() the job of returning whatever mem_block points at is right for a
block the DMA still owns, and wrong for one the RX callback has already handed to the
receive queue -- because mem_block goes on pointing at it. Six lines below that
k_msgq_put(), the STOPPING branch does `goto rx_disable`, which lands in
rx_stop_transfer(), which frees the block the queue is holding. i2s_read() then returns a
block that is already on the free list and frees it a second time.

The leak had been covering for this one too.

Measured with the RX hand-off removed -- the mechanism, step by step, in the census:

    c3.stop      tx=6 rx=7    one RX block in flight, queue empty
    c3.stopped   tx=7 rx=8    rx_stop_transfer() freed the block the QUEUE owns  (7 -> 8)
    c3.tail-done tx=7 rx=9    i2s_read() freed the same block again              (8 -> 9)
    *** STRESS: SLAB CORRUPT at cycle 4: tx=8 rx=9 of 8. ***

Nine free blocks in a slab of eight: the same block is on the free list twice.

The fix is to hand ownership over explicitly -- NULL mem_block at the instant the queue
takes the block -- after which every path out of the callback is correct by construction
rather than by accident:

    queue full     mem_block still ours        -> rx_stop_transfer() frees it   (right)
    STOPPING       mem_block NULL, queue owns  -> rx_stop_transfer() skips it   (right)
    alloc failed   k_mem_slab_alloc() NULLs it -> rx_stop_transfer() skips it   (right)
    dma failed     freed and NULLed inline     -> rx_stop_transfer() skips it   (right)

The alloc-failure row is worth spelling out, because a reviewer raised it as a second
double-free trigger and it is not one -- but only by luck. It is safe TODAY because
k_mem_slab_alloc() writes NULL through its out-parameter when a K_NO_WAIT allocation
fails (kernel/mem_slab.c). That is an implementation detail of the allocator, not a
documented contract. With the hand-off above, that path no longer depends on it.

AND THE PART WORTH REMEMBERING
------------------------------
Bug 1 was found by eye, not by the harness, and the harness then told me -- in capital
letters, in a log I committed -- that it had found it. Two bugs on the same code path,
each masking the other, behind a green test.

The harness now asserts the one thing it was only printing. Both directions are verified
below: it fails on the tx_stop build and passes on the correct one.

